---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-epilepsy`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Epilepsy',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Epilepsy', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'G40', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Epilepsy', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Epilepsy: Why Seizure Medicines Drain Your Levels"
  description="Many antiseizure medications accelerate vitamin D breakdown, making deficiency and bone loss common in epilepsy. Why it happens, who is at risk, and what monitoring makes sense."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-epilepsy", label: "Vitamin D & Epilepsy" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Epilepsy</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      The strongest connection between vitamin D and epilepsy is not that vitamin D prevents seizures —
      it is that the drugs used to prevent seizures deplete vitamin D. Long-term antiseizure medication
      is one of the best-documented causes of drug-induced vitamin D deficiency and bone loss, and it is
      often overlooked because the effect builds silently over years.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">How antiseizure drugs lower vitamin D</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Several older antiseizure medications — phenytoin, phenobarbital, primidone, and carbamazepine —
      are <strong>enzyme inducers</strong>: they ramp up liver cytochrome-P450 enzymes that accelerate
      the breakdown of 25(OH)D into inactive metabolites. The result is a faster clearance of vitamin D
      and lower circulating levels for any given intake. Valproate works through different mechanisms
      but is also associated with reduced bone density. Over years, this contributes to osteomalacia,
      osteoporosis, and increased fracture risk in people with epilepsy — sometimes called
      "anticonvulsant osteomalacia." See our broader
      <a href="/vitamin-d-drug-interactions" class="text-amber-700 underline">drug interactions page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Who is most at risk</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>People on enzyme-inducing drugs (phenytoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine, primidone), especially long-term.</li>
      <li>Those on multiple antiseizure drugs (polytherapy).</li>
      <li>People with additional risk factors: limited sun exposure, institutional living, immobility, or dark skin at high latitude.</li>
      <li>Children and adolescents accruing peak bone mass, and older adults already losing it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Does vitamin D reduce seizures?</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      This is a separate and much weaker question. A few small, mostly uncontrolled studies have
      suggested that correcting vitamin D deficiency might modestly reduce seizure frequency in some
      patients, and vitamin D receptors are present in the brain. But there is no robust randomised
      evidence that vitamin D is an anticonvulsant, and it should never be used in place of prescribed
      seizure medication. The clear, actionable benefit is protecting bone, not controlling seizures.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sensible monitoring</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Because the depletion is predictable, many neurologists check 25(OH)D in people on long-term
      antiseizure medication and supplement to keep it in the sufficient range, sometimes at higher
      doses than usual to counter the accelerated clearance. Bone density assessment is reasonable after
      years of therapy, particularly with enzyme-inducing drugs. None of this should be improvised
      without input from the team managing the epilepsy, since dose adjustments interact with the wider
      treatment plan.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Never change or stop antiseizure medication on your own —
      abrupt withdrawal can trigger seizures. Discuss vitamin D and bone monitoring with your neurology
      team as part of long-term care.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-epilepsy" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
